<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Ifran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ifranids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Ifran were a Zenata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. In the Berber revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> from 740 they embraced Sufri Kharijism, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-qurra">Abu Qurra</a> founded a state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. In the 10th and 11th centuries Ifranid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled Tlemcen and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a>, at times allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> conquered their lands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, the tribe took its name from an ancestor, Ifri, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> word meaning cavern. The oldest mentions place most of the Banu Ifran in the west of Mauretania Caesariensis, and they were one of the four major tribes of the Zenata confederation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès Mountains</a>. Ibn Khaldun says they resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corippus">Corippus</a> records them challenging the Byzantine armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a> between 547 and 550. In the 7th century they sided with the Kahina against the Umayyad invaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> the Banu Ifran lived around Yafran in Tripolitania. The conquests probably pushed them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a>, and an Abbasid invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 761 further west. Their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-qurra">Abu Qurra</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 765 over the site of the Roman city of Pomaria. In the 10th century they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>; led by Abu Yazid they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in 945, and Ya'la ibn Mohammed took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and built a capital at Ifgan near Mascara until the Fatimid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> killed him in 954 and destroyed it.</p><p>Some Ifranids then settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, where the house of Corra ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, and others held Tadla and built a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a>. Ya'la's son Yaddū took Fes in January 993, and his grandson Tamīm took it again in 1033; Tamīm began a persecution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and is said to have killed 6,000 of their men, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> speaks of persecution without killing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> fell in 1058, and in 1066 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> under Ibn Tashfin took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and ended the Banu Ifran's power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Banu Ifran were known as expert cavalrymen. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> animism was their main belief, and Ifri was also the name of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> deity, a sun and cave goddess and protector of the home whose rites were held in caves for the protection of merchants. After conversion they joined the Kharijite movement, opposing the Sunni Arab armies, and in the 8th century they rose around the Sufri doctrine against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Ifran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Banu Ifran</b> were a Zenata Berber tribe of western Algeria.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1065 al-Mutadid of the Taifa of Seville annexed Ronda, ending <b>Ifranid</b> rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-ronda">Taifa of Ronda</a></li></ul></section>
